- TODO: statistical review of Chapter 8 - From dice to A/B tests
    - Date: 2026-04-30
    - Summary
        - Chapter 8 walks two-arm A/B mechanics through five loops (binary, continuous, stratification, decision rule, PyMC). The mechanics are sound and the two-lens framing is consistent with the rest of the project. Two rounds of critic-defender review surfaced one numerical claim that does not match the simulator, several missing caveats around the choice of test (pooled vs unpooled z, Welch vs Student t), an honesty issue around how MMU is framed, an under-explained directional alpha inflation, and a missing mention of the canonical CMH stratified test. None of these break the chapter, but each is a one- to three-line edit.
    - Round 1
        - Critic
            - C1. Loop D claims "frequentist ships about 25% of the time" at true effect = 0.5pp (right at the MMU). Re-running the simulator at N=2000 per arm, baseline 0.10, true_diff=0.005, n_runs=1000 gives F ship rate near 0.07, not 0.25. The notebook output for the same scenario also prints 0.07. The "25%" claim looks like a stale number or a different baseline.
            - C2. expkit.inference.normal.two_proportion_z uses the pooled p_hat for the SE. That is the correct default for testing H0: p1 = p2, but it is not what most A/B power-and-sample-size calculators use (those use the unpooled SE under the alternative). The chapter never names pooled vs unpooled, so a reader who later compares against power.binomial output or against a vendor calculator will see a small mismatch and not know why.
            - C3. Loop B uses Welch's t (equal_var=False) without comment. Welch is a reasonable default, but the choice between Welch and Student t-with-pooled-variance is one of the live choices a practitioner makes. The chapter should at least say "we use Welch because it doesn't assume equal variance, and revenue tails commonly differ between arms".
            - C4. MMU (minimum meaningful uplift) is presented as a Bayesian rule. It is not. MMU is an organizational/economic choice about what effect size matters; it can be bolted onto either lens (frequentist TOST-style equivalence tests, or "ship if lower 95% CI bound > MMU" use the same idea). Framing MMU as a Bayesian invention misleads the reader and undersells frequentist practice.
            - C5. The directional shipping rule "ship if p < 0.05 AND direction positive" effectively halves the alpha that matters for shipping. The actual one-sided false-ship rate at truth = 0 is alpha/2 = 0.025, confirmed by simulation (~0.026). The chapter says "alpha = 0.05 minus the directional half", which is opaque. Spell it out: "the rule's effective one-sided alpha is 0.025".
            - C6. Loop C's stratification analysis stops at per-stratum z-tests + a pooled bar chart. The standard frequentist tool for combining strata while preserving the stratification is the Cochran-Mantel-Haenszel (CMH) test. Not mentioning CMH, even as a one-liner pointer, leaves a gap a real practitioner will notice.
        - Defender
            - On C1: accepted. The "25%" number is wrong and contradicts both generate.py's own simulation and the notebook's printed output. Edit the chapter prose to "frequentist ships about 7% of the time, Bayesian about 5%". Optionally add a sentence: "the two rules differ less than you'd expect because at this N the noise floor swamps the MMU bookkeeping; the gap widens at smaller N or larger MMU".
            - On C2: accepted as a small honest aside. Add one sentence in Loop A: "we use the pooled-SE form of the two-proportion z because we are testing the null p1 = p2; sample-size calculators often use the unpooled form under the alternative, so the two will not match exactly". One sentence; no algebra.
            - On C3: accepted. Add to Loop B: "we use Welch's t (no equal-variance assumption) because revenue per user typically has heavier tails in one arm than the other; Student's t with pooled variance is fine when variances are known to match".
            - On C4: accepted, this is the most important edit. Reframe MMU in Loop D as an organizational choice that can be added to either lens. Show the frequentist version as well: "ship if the lower bound of the 95% CI exceeds MMU" (a one-sided non-inferiority style rule). This also fixes the "frequentist isn't aware there is a meaningful threshold" hunch, which currently overstates the case.
            - On C5: accepted. Replace "alpha = 0.05 minus the directional half" with "the rule is effectively one-sided at alpha = 0.025; under truth = 0 the rule ships about 2.5% of the time". Cleaner and closer to what the simulation actually shows.
            - On C6: accepted as a forward pointer, not a full treatment. Add one line in Loop C's hunch: "the canonical frequentist combiner is Cochran-Mantel-Haenszel; chapters 11 and 12 will revisit the question with hierarchical models". Avoid full CMH mechanics here -- this chapter is about A/B mechanics, not stratified analysis.
    - Round 2
        - Critic
            - C7. After accepting the MMU reframing, the Loop D figure caption ("Loop E: shipping rates differ when truth is between 0 and MMU") is misleading: under the new reframing the freq lens can also have an MMU rule, so the figure is comparing one specific freq rule to one specific Bayes rule, not freq-vs-Bayes in general. Update the figure title and the surrounding prose to make that clear.
            - C8. Loop A's Bayesian posterior uses a Beta(1,1) prior on each arm. The chapter never says so; the reader has to read generate.py to find out. State the prior in chapter.md (one sentence). Same for Loop F. This is a "carry the math forward" issue that already bit Chapter 6 reviewers.
            - C9. The bootstrap CI in Loop B is computed on the difference of means and reported as [0.13, 0.86]. The hand-rolled bootstrap was already replaced by expkit.inference.bootstrap.bootstrap_diff_ci (good, ROADMAP Area 8 already done for this chapter). But the chapter never says how many bootstrap resamples (4000) or that the percentile method is used vs BCa. A one-line footnote: "4000 percentile-method resamples; for skewed metrics, BCa is preferred and lives in expkit.inference.bootstrap".
            - C10. The "Bayesian false-positive rate depends on prior + threshold" claim at truth = 0 is correct but vague. With Beta(1,1) priors and the "P(diff > MMU=0.005) > 0.95" rule at truth = 0, the simulated false-ship rate is small (the notebook shows 0.01 at truth = 0). State the actual number from the simulation rather than waving at "it depends".
        - Defender
            - On C7: accepted. Update the figure suptitle and the Loop D prose to "freq with H0:diff=0 vs Bayes with MMU" and add a sentence "if you give the freq lens an MMU too -- via a one-sided non-inferiority CI -- the two lenses converge". No need to actually plot the freq-with-MMU curve; just note it.
            - On C8: accepted. Add "uniform Beta(1,1) priors on each arm" to Loop A and Loop F. One sentence each.
            - On C9: accepted, but keep it brief. Add one footnote-style line in Loop B: "bootstrap is 4000 percentile resamples; BCa is available in expkit.inference.bootstrap for skewed metrics".
            - On C10: accepted. Replace "depends on prior + threshold" with the actual simulated rate ("about 1% at truth = 0 with Beta(1,1) priors and MMU = 0.5pp"). Concrete numbers always beat a hedge.
